  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nHigh-amplitude upper ridging will keep fair weather and near\nnormal temperatures in place over the next couple of days. But \nanother southern stream disturbance will bring the risk of rain \nshowers to the area again late Friday into Saturday.\n\nThis system looks to move into northern Baja Friday afternoon,\nwith a slight negative tilt orientation that will enhance upper\nlevel diffluence out ahead of it. This should result in light \nrain showers developing by Friday afternoon in SW New Mexico, \ndespite the stronger forcing for lift lagging back to the west. \n\n700 mb temperatures will drop to around the freezing mark as the\nmain trough (or loosely-closed mid level low) moves overhead\nSaturday morning. Some rain/snow mix is possible in the highest\nelevations of the Sacramento Mountains again, with perhaps some\nlight accumulations above 9500 feet again by Saturday morning. \n\nPrecip chances will hang back over the Sacramento Mountains\nSaturday morning, before pulling out to the east. The main trough\nsteering this system will keep precip chances going in northern\nNew Mexico, and we'll likely see clouds hanging back over the\nhigher terrain in our area.",
